1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,370
Penny wants a jacket that costs a hundred and thirty-five dollars.

2
00:00:04,690 --> 00:00:09,109
At checkout, a button offers to split it into four easy payments.

3
00:00:09,429 --> 00:00:10,730
No interest.

4
00:00:11,050 --> 00:00:12,460
No credit card.

5
00:00:12,779 --> 00:00:18,350
Just tap. So if Penny pays zero interest, how does the lender make any money?

6
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:23,219
Last episode, subscriptions charged Penny a little every month.

7
00:00:23,539 --> 00:00:26,100
Buy now, pay later looks like the opposite:

8
00:00:26,420 --> 00:00:28,399
a loan that costs nothing.

9
00:00:28,719 --> 00:00:34,039
Here are the three engines that make "free" credit a multi-billion-dollar business.

10
00:00:34,689 --> 00:00:36,219
And it took off fast.

11
00:00:36,539 --> 00:00:42,320
In twenty nineteen, the five biggest lenders made about seventeen million of these loans.

12
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:46,060
Two years later, it was a hundred and eighty million.

13
00:00:46,380 --> 00:00:50,770
Something that grows that fast is making somebody a lot of money.

14
00:00:51,420 --> 00:00:53,149
Engine number one:

15
00:00:53,469 --> 00:00:54,729
the shop pays.

16
00:00:55,049 --> 00:01:00,210
When Penny uses pay later, the lender pays the shop right away, minus a cut.

17
00:01:00,530 --> 00:01:03,090
And that cut is bigger than a card's.

18
00:01:03,409 --> 00:01:10,430
In a government study of the biggest lenders, the shop's fee averaged about two and a half percent of each sale.

19
00:01:10,750 --> 00:01:15,040
The comparable card fee in that same study was under one percent.

20
00:01:15,689 --> 00:01:17,590
So why would a shop pay more?

21
00:01:17,909 --> 00:01:22,280
Because people who can split the price tend to buy more, and buy bigger.

22
00:01:22,599 --> 00:01:27,609
The lender is selling the shop Penny's willingness to spend money she doesn't have yet.

23
00:01:28,260 --> 00:01:30,010
Engine number two:

24
00:01:30,329 --> 00:01:36,090
late fees. The interest-free deal only works if every payment lands on time.

25
00:01:36,409 --> 00:01:41,170
Miss one, and many lenders charge a late fee, often around seven dollars.

26
00:01:41,489 --> 00:01:46,480
In twenty twenty-one, about one in ten borrowers was charged at least one.

27
00:01:47,129 --> 00:01:50,120
And a missed payment can cost more than that fee.

28
00:01:50,439 --> 00:01:55,129
The lender usually takes payments automatically from Penny's debit card.

29
00:01:55,450 --> 00:01:59,079
If her account is empty, it can try again, and again.

30
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:04,709
Regulators found some lenders retried a single payment up to eight times.

31
00:02:05,030 --> 00:02:09,020
Each failed attempt can trigger a fee at Penny's bank as well.

32
00:02:09,669 --> 00:02:11,439
Engine number three:

33
00:02:11,759 --> 00:02:14,789
the habit. Each loan feels tiny.

34
00:02:15,110 --> 00:02:17,139
Thirty-three dollars here.

35
00:02:17,460 --> 00:02:19,120
Twenty dollars there.

36
00:02:19,439 --> 00:02:21,979
So it's easy to have several at once.

37
00:02:22,300 --> 00:02:31,340
In twenty twenty-two, sixty-three percent of borrowers took out more than one of these loans during the year, and a third used more than one lender.

38
00:02:31,990 --> 00:02:39,909
The big lenders made two hundred and seventy-seven million of these loans in a single year, worth thirty-four billion dollars.

39
00:02:40,229 --> 00:02:44,500
And nearly two thirds of them went to people with lower credit scores.

40
00:02:44,819 --> 00:02:50,430
The customers most likely to stack payments are the ones with the least room for a slip.

41
00:02:51,080 --> 00:02:52,719
Here's how it sneaks up.

42
00:02:53,039 --> 00:02:56,319
Each plan usually takes a payment every two weeks.

43
00:02:56,639 --> 00:02:58,860
One plan is easy to track.

44
00:02:59,180 --> 00:03:03,919
Four plans, from three different apps, can all land in the same week as rent.

45
00:03:04,240 --> 00:03:07,139
Nothing about any single loan is scary.

46
00:03:07,460 --> 00:03:09,189
It's the pile that hurts.

47
00:03:09,840 --> 00:03:11,550
So here's the machine.

48
00:03:11,870 --> 00:03:14,189
The shop pays to get you spending.

49
00:03:14,509 --> 00:03:16,539
Late fees punish the slip.

50
00:03:16,860 --> 00:03:18,990
And the habit keeps you coming back.

51
00:03:19,310 --> 00:03:24,729
Pay later isn't bad on its own, but it's still debt, even when it feels like shopping.

52
00:03:25,050 --> 00:03:29,719
One: only use it for things you could buy today with cash.

53
00:03:30,039 --> 00:03:34,409
Two: never have more than one plan running at a time.

54
00:03:34,729 --> 00:03:40,389
Three: put every due date in your calendar, and keep the money in your account.

55
00:03:41,039 --> 00:03:43,789
Pay later makes small debts feel free.

56
00:03:44,110 --> 00:03:47,990
But for people with the least money, almost everything costs more.

57
00:03:48,310 --> 00:03:52,430
Why being broke is so expensive, next on Money Mechanics.

